findings.description:
HEREDOC is `<<-?\s*(['"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\1[^
]*
(?:.*?
)*?\s*\2...` and strip_heredocs replaces the whole match with `<<HEREDOC`. The `[^
]*` after the delimiter consumes the remainder of the operator line, so everything written after `<<WORD` on that line is removed from `body` before the ABS_WORD scan: redirect targets (`cat <<'EOF' > /root/notes.md`), pipe stages (`cat <<EOF | tee /mnt/out`) and positional arguments (`python3 - <<'PY' /srv/data`). The header documents 1.2.2 as stripping heredoc *bodies*; the operator line is not body. The same write with the operands reordered (`cat > /root/notes.md <<'EOF'`) is still flagged, so the verdict depends on operand order.

findings.suggested_fix:
Capture the rest of the operator line and re-emit it: e.g. `HEREDOC = re.compile(r"(<<-?\s*(['"]?)([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)\2)([^
]*
)(?:[^
]*
)*?[ 	]*\3[ 	]*(?=
|$)")` and `HEREDOC.sub(lambda m: "<<HEREDOC" + m.group(4), val)`. Add a grader bats case for `cat <<'EOF' > /root/x` and `cat <<EOF | tee /mnt/x` expecting outside_path.

Drop re.S and make each body iteration consume exactly one line: `(?:[^
]*
)*?` with the terminator anchored as `[ 	]*\2[ 	]*(?=
|$)`. Without re.S the tiling is unambiguous and matching is linear. The fix in DISS-001 already uses this shape.
